<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Options &amp; Upgrades</a> / 01</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">CUSTOMIZATION TOPIC 01 \u00b7 Cost &amp; Site</div><h1>Options &amp; Upgrades Pricing Overview</h1><p>Compare choices against an approved model and size before requesting a written price.</p></div><section class=\"asi-cost-review\" id=\"option-planning-prices\"><h2>Option pricing &amp; budget planning</h2><p class=\"asi-cost-alert\"><strong>Research-stage planning references:</strong> The figures below come from historical ASI planning material or approved model siding formulas. Historical amounts have NOT been verified as current Appalachian Sheds selling prices. They are not quotes. Final prices and any credits for included standard features must appear in a written proposal.</p><table class=\"asi-cost-list\"><thead><tr><th>Option / scope</th><th>Planning figure</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Typical wooden access ramp</td><td>$450\u2013$900</td><td>per ramp, legacy ASI planning</td></tr><tr><td>Additional aluminum single-hung window</td><td>$235\u2013$335</td><td>per unit, legacy reference</td></tr><tr><td>Small decorative cupola</td><td>$900\u2013$1,750</td><td>installed, legacy reference</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"asi-cost-note\">Standard ASI building features remain included in the approved model price. Figures shown are for planning context only; these are not added to the online shed calculator. No site-specific price should be inferred from a price per linear or square foot.</p><a href=\"/options-gallery/\">Explore visual option comparisons</a></section></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>The best building is not the one with the most upgrades. It is the one where the site, structure, roof, openings, comfort systems, utilities, and finishes were selected in the right order for the way the building will actually serve you.</p><p>This Center is the simple front door to Appalachian Sheds' complete Options &amp; Upgrades program. Start with your intended use, review the decisions that are hardest to change later, and then open only the option categories that apply to your project.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>The essentials</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The best building is not the one with the most upgrades. It is the one where the site, structure, roof, openings, comfort systems, utilities, and finishes were selected in the right order for the way the building will actually serve you.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>This Center is the simple front door to Appalachian Sheds' complete Options &amp; Upgrades program. Start with your intended use, review the decisions that are hardest to change later, and then open only the option categories that apply to your project.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Detailed specifications and planning ranges remain on the individual option pages so the same pricing is not repeated across multiple parts of the website.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>What Will This Building Actually Need to Do?</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A storage shed, workshop, backyard office, pool house, and premium retreat should not receive the same option package. Intended use is the quickest way to narrow the choices before detailed design begins.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Start with the use that is closest to your project. You can always add or remove categories as the design develops.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>Upgrade Mistakes Are Usually Timing Mistakes.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The most expensive change is often a good option chosen after the best installation point has already passed. Use this sequence to keep planning logical.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Placement, drainage, pad, foundation, buried utilities, and access.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Floor system, wall depth, doors, windows, lofts, porches, and structural roof features.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-4\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 04</div><h2>Site, Base &amp; Floor</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>These are the earliest decisions because they determine how the building bears on the property, how water is managed, and how the floor performs over time.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Site Preparation &amp; Building Pad</h3><p>Clearing, grade, drainage, stone preparation, geotextile, and the ASI moisture-control pad system.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Foundation &amp; Base Options</h3><p>Support, leveling, separation from ground conditions, and compatibility with the selected building.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Flooring &amp; Structural Floor Options</h3><p>Subfloor systems, pressure-treated upgrades, heavy-use floors, and finished-floor readiness.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Floor Insulation</h3><p>Protected batt systems, mineral wool, closed-cell spray foam, and premium insulated-floor packages.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-5\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 05</div><h2>Structure, Exterior &amp; Roof</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>These choices define the building shell, weather protection, architectural character, maintenance expectations, and long-term durability.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Construction Materials &amp; Structural Standards</h3><p>Framing, pressure-treated lumber, plywood and OSB distinctions, siding backing, fasteners, and connector standards.</p><p>Current ASI siding systems, backing requirements, WRB coordination, and exterior appearance.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Paint, Stain &amp; Exterior Finish Systems</h3><p>Paint systems, natural-wood finishes, stains, trim accents, and substrate-compatible finish planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Roofing &amp; Roofline Upgrades</h3><p>Roofing materials, underlayment, flashing, soffit, fascia, and primary roof-system upgrades.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Metal Roofing vs. Architectural Shingles</h3><p>A focused comparison of cost, sound, maintenance, appearance, service life, and ownership priorities.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-6\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 06</div><h2>Openings, Daylight &amp; Access</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>These decisions affect daily movement, natural light, equipment access, security, views, ventilation, and the framing of the building itself.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Doors &amp; Entry Systems</h3><p>Fiberglass and specialty doors, double-door systems, glazing, hardware, weather protection, and equipment access.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Windows &amp; Natural Light</h3><p>Window type, size, placement, ventilation, privacy, daylight, and finished-room layout.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Skylights &amp; Overhead Daylight</h3><p>Fixed, venting, powered, tubular, and curb-mounted skylight systems with flashing and light-shaft planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Ramps, Steps, Landings &amp; Access</h3><p>Equipment ramps, steps, landings, threshold transitions, and practical access planning.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-7\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 07</div><h2>Function, Storage &amp; Architectural Character</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>These options improve usefulness, storage capacity, exterior presence, and property fit after the primary structure and openings are understood.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Lofts, Ladders &amp; Stairs</h3><p>Storage lofts, access ladders, and stair systems coordinated with framing, headroom, and lighting.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Shelving, Workbenches &amp; Storage</h3><p>Built-ins, work surfaces, wall organization, and storage layouts coordinated with doors, windows, and utilities.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Porches, Awnings, Dormers &amp; Architectural Roofline</h3><p>Porches, entry canopies, dormers, gable overhangs, and integrated architectural roofline features.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Fascia &amp; Trim Packages</h3><p>Rake, fascia, soffit, trim depth, and detail combinations that complete the roofline and exterior.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-8\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 08</div><h2>Comfort, Interior &amp; Utilities</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>These categories turn a basic building into a workshop, office, studio, pool house, retreat, or premium finished flex space.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Electrical, Lighting, HVAC &amp; Comfort Systems</h3><p>Electrical service, outlets, lighting, mini-splits, technology pathways, and comfort-system readiness.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Interior Insulation, Wall &amp; Ceiling Systems</h3><p>Thermal performance, sound control, wall and ceiling systems, and finish readiness.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Interior Finish Packages</h3><p>Utility interiors, office and studio packages, drywall, plywood, T&amp;G, shiplap, beadboard, and trim.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Plumbing, Water &amp; Utility Readiness</h3><p>Water supply, drains, utility routes, frost protection, rough-in sequencing, and licensed-trade coordination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-9\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 09</div><h2>One Price Should Have One Authoritative Home.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The Options Center intentionally does not repeat detailed pricing from every category. Each individual option page owns its current planning ranges, scope notes, and price drivers.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>This prevents the same option from showing one price on the Center, another price on a category page, and a third price inside a calculator.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-10\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 10</div><h2>All 25 Options &amp; Upgrades Categories</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>If you already know what you are looking for, use this complete list to go directly to the relevant category.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-11\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 11</div><h2>Design the Building Before Construction Begins.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The goal is not to sell every upgrade. The goal is to build the right structure for your property, intended use, and long-term plans.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section verified-planning\" id=\"verified-planning\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">CURRENT PLANNING GUIDANCE</div><h2>Written proposal controls the final options price</h2><p>The five approved model siding tiers are DuraTemp, LP SmartSide Vertical Textured Panel, LP SmartSide Board &amp; Batten, LP SmartSide Lap and GP Vinyl Lap. Not every specialty model necessarily supports every configuration without a technical review.</p><p>Painting, electrical work, custom glazing, structural changes and interior improvements are separate optional scope items unless included in the written proposal. No historic Divi estimate should be treated as a current approved price.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Compare this decision with the relevant model features and options, then request a written proposal identifying standard inclusions, any optional work and property-specific requirements.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"references\"><h2>Relevant planning references</h2><p>These official and manufacturer resources provide additional background. Product and jurisdiction requirements must be checked for the exact project.</p><p><a href=\"https://bsesc.energy.gov/energy-basics/site-excavated-final-grade-sloped-away-home\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">U.S. DOE: site grading and drainage</a></p><p><a href=\"https://bsesc.energy.gov/energy-basics/foundation-drain-gutters-downspouts\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">U.S. DOE: runoff around foundations</a></p></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#verified-planning\">Current planning guidance</a><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">The essentials</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">What Will This Building Actually Need to Do?</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">Upgrade Mistakes Are Usually Timing Mistakes.</a><a href=\"#topic-4\">Site, Base &amp; Floor</a><a href=\"#topic-5\">Structure, Exterior &amp; Roof</a><a href=\"#topic-6\">Openings, Daylight &amp; Access</a><a href=\"#topic-7\">Function, Storage &amp; Architectural Character</a><a href=\"#topic-8\">Comfort, Interior &amp; Utilities</a><a href=\"#topic-9\">One Price Should Have One Authoritative Home.</a><a href=\"#topic-10\">All 25 Options &amp; Upgrades Categories</a><a href=\"#topic-11\">Design the Building Before Construction Begins.</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a href=\"#references\">Planning references</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

